  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /app\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "file mystery\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery; echo \"EXIT=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The mystery binary prints a series of messages: 'Creating high-resolution image: 800x600', downsampling note, rendering, progress 99.8%, and 'Rendering complete'. It seems to render an image. I need to see if it produces any output files and whether it's deterministic. Let me investigate further.",
  "plan": "Run the binary again and check what files it produces. Examine strings in the binary to understand its logic. Check for any output files generated. Look for dependencies (ffmpeg).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery; echo \"EXIT=$?\"; ls -la\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The mystery program prints several status messages and generates image.ppm (5,362,624 bytes). For 800x600 image, 800*600*3 = 1,440,000 bytes, so the file is much larger - likely a higher internal resolution or different format. I need to understand the exact rendering algorithm to reproduce it byte-identically. Let me examine the PPM header and check available reverse-engineering tools.",
  "plan": "Examine the image.ppm header to understand dimensions. Check for decompilation tools (objdump, gdb, strings). Then analyze the binary to understand the rendering algorithm.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 64 image.ppm | xxd | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which objdump gdb strings nm readelf python3 ffmpeg 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "No xxd, but I have od, objdump, strings, nm, readelf, python3. No ffmpeg (the program mentions ffmpeg but may not actually call it, or it might be a stub). Let me examine the PPM header and the binary's strings.",
  "plan": "Look at the PPM header using od to understand dimensions and format. Then extract strings from the binary to learn the algorithm details (likely messages and possibly function names).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 32 image.ppm | od -c | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings -n 4 mystery | head -80\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Now I understand the structure: mystery writes a P3 (ASCII) PPM file 'image.ppm' of 800x600 with maxval 255. It uses sqrt, fmax, fmin from libm. The exact pixel computation must be replicated. I need to decompile to find the rendering algorithm. Let me check symbol names and disassemble.",
  "plan": "First run nm to get function symbols. Then disassemble the relevant functions (likely main and helpers computing pixel colors). I'll analyze the algorithm to reproduce it exactly.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm mystery | grep -i ' T \\| t ' | head -40\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm mystery | grep -i ' [Tt] '\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "This is a 3D ray tracer written in C. Symbols reveal: vector_* math functions, ray_create/ray_at, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, is_in_shadow, sky_color, trace, allocate_image, write_image, and main. It writes an 800x600 P3 ASCII PPM (image.ppm). To reproduce byte-identical output I must reconstruct the exact algorithm and floating-point operations. I'll disassemble the key functions.",
  "plan": "Disassemble the binary to a file with Intel syntax, then read the key functions: main, trace, sky_color, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, is_in_shadow, write_image, allocate_image. This will reveal the scene constants and shading model.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "objdump -d -M intel mystery > /tmp/disasm.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -n '<main>:' /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

